% Introduction. Under the S2 cold-start setting, where both drugs are unseen at test time, the transferable DDI signal is intrinsically low-rank, with rank saturation appearing early and consistently across tasks and backbones.

% Gap. Low-rank is necessary but not sufficient, because an unconstrained full-capacity model may allocate that limited subspace to dominant or higher-variance structure rather than to the transferable invariant directions, and prior work on invariant-feature subspace recovery shows that such invariant directions need not coincide with top-variance directions and therefore may require explicit recovery.

% Motivation. Once the transferable signal is low-rank and the S2 setting prohibits drug-identity cues, the representation should be built from a compact identity-free basis grounded in node-level KG semantics, which motivates a typed prototype space routed by semantic information.

% Method. The proposed method instantiates this design as a semantically-typed low-rank adaptation over a KG-structure prototype space, with typed prototype bases in $M_B$ and pair-conditioned block-sparse routing in $M_A^{(u,v)}$ driven by frozen ontology text embeddings $z_m$, cold-start safe by construction.

% Experiments. Across diverse backbones, this design yields consistent gains on the S2 cold-start setting.

% Analysis. The analysis then establishes the mechanism through spectral quantification of the low-rank structure, component ablations, within-type semantic shuffling as a causal control, and biological attribution that highlights the PD mechanism class where structure-only models fail and semantic routing recovers performance.

